    "verification": "abstract-read",
    "safeflowSummary": "This landmark US hospital study linked higher patient-to-nurse ratios to higher risk-adjusted 30-day mortality and a higher rate of failure-to-rescue - death following a complication that might have been caught and escalated in time. Each additional patient per nurse also raised the odds of nurse burnout and job dissatisfaction, outcomes that plausibly feed back into how reliably deterioration gets noticed and escalated. SafeFlow references this study to ground the idea that safe escalation and staffing levels are linked, not purely a matter of individual vigilance."
  },
